Notes on Recode File

1.   To merge the data in this standard recode file and the service
     availability data from the clusters, use the coding found in
     locations 2 through 7 of the CASEID variable.

2.   The variables HV004, V004 and MV004 (Ultimate area unit) are
     taken from the cluster number originally assigned by the DHS
     sampler.

3.   In the household data, HV222 is not applicable.  Please see
     SH29A, SH29B and SH29C for data about salt in the household.

4.   In the individual woman's data, V110 and V111 are not
     applicable.  Please see S116 and S117 for information about
     exposure to radio and television.

5.   There are 3 cases in which V133 (total years of education) is
     only 4 years less than V012 (age of the respondent).

6.   There are 2 cases in which V135 (visitor/resident) is missing.

7.   There are 138 cases for which a response of "don't know" has
     been corrected to "no" for V398.  The original responses can
     be found in country-specific variable S340.

8.   There is 1 case in which a code of "2" for V447 has been
     corrected to "6" (other).

9.   There are 4 children whose health card was seen (H1 equals 1)
     but no vaccinations were recorded on the card.

10.  There are 5 cases in which V602 and V525 are both missing.

11.  The base for V723 (caretaker for child) is working women who
     have a child 3 years of age or younger, living at home with
     the mother.  The standard base is working women with a child
     under the age of 5.

12.  There are 23 cases in which the code of 9999995 is used in
     V738 since the amount of income earned was greater than the
     size of the field.

13.  There are 3 cases in which the number of siblings of the
     respondent is missing.  There are no occurrences of REC83
     (maternal mortality) for those cases.

14.  In REC83 (maternal mortality), there are 360 siblings who are
     coded missing for MM1 (sex of sibling).  There are 96 siblings
     who are coded missing for MM2 (survival status for sibling).

Notes on couples (linking the male and female recode files)

In linking the couples that can be found in the women's and men's
recode files, there are some mismatches.  Only couples for which
there is consistency in reporting should be used for analysis.

1.   There are 48 cases in which the husband reported in V034 of
     the women's recode file is not in the men's recode file
     because he was not eligible.

2.   There are 44 cases in which the husband reported in V034 of
     the women's recode file is in the men's recode file, but he
     does not have a complete questionnaire.

3.   There are 10 cases in which the husband reported in V034 of
     the women's recode file is in the men's recode file, but he
     does not report the woman as his wife in his variable MV034.

4.   There are 131 cases in which the wife reported in MV034 of the
     men's recode file is not in the women's recode file because
     she was not eligible.

5.   There are 42 cases in which the wife reported in MV034 of the
     men's recode file is in the women's recode file, but she does
     not have a complete questionnaire.

6.   There are 16 cases in which the wife reported in MV034 of the
     men's recode file is in the women's recode file, but she does
     not report the man as her husband in her variable V034.

Inconsistencies in the Data File

1.   There are 6 households with no adults; there are 3 households
     with no dejure members.

2.   There are 147 cases in which HV105 (age of household member)
     is less than 5 years, but HV106 (education level) is not 0.

3.   There are 5 cases in which the V031 (field editor) in the
     individual file is not the same code as HV031.

4.   There is one case in which V034 (husband's line number)
     identifies a male in the household who is less than 10 years
     of age.

5.   There are 22 cases in which V148 (attending school) differs
     from HV110 (attending school) in the household data.

6.   There are 3 cases in which V108 (literacy) is missing.  In one
     case, V109 (reads newspaper) is also missing.  In the other 2
     cases, V109 equals 1 (yes).

7.   There are 2 cases in which the respondent was a visitor who
     usually lived abroad (V139), but provided a region in Benin
     for residence.  The data in V140 and V141 were corrected to be
     consistent.

8.   There are 2 births for which B6 (age at death) was reported as
     zero years.  B7 (imputed age at death was calculated as zero
     months.

9.   There are 54 births for which B6 (age at death) was reported
     as one year.

10.  There are 10 cases in which V215 exceeds the interval since
     the last birth and the cases are flagged.

11.  There are 2 cases in which V215 (time since last menstruation)
     equals 996 (never menstruated), but the respondent has given
     birth.  There is one case in which V215 equals 995 (before
     last birth), but the respondent has never given birth.

12.  There is 1 case in which the respondent reported 2 children
     when first using contraception (V310), but she has had no live
     births.

13.  There are 3 cases in which the respondent reported ever use of
     male sterilization, but is currently using no contraceptive
     method.

14.  There is 1 child with an out-of-range value in the raw data
     for M17.  The value in M17 has been corrected to "no".

15.  There are cases with inconsistencies between M37 variables
     (child given particular foods) and M40 variables (foods given
     in last 7 days).  Specifically:

          2 births for which M40A is inconsistent with M37A.
          86 births for which M40B is inconsistent with M37E, M37F
          and/or M37G.
          62 births for which M40C is inconsistent with M37B, M37C,
          M37D and/or M37L.
          10 births for which M40D is inconsistent with M37Q.
          7 births for which M40E is inconsistent with M37R.
          3 births for which M40F is inconsistent with M37S.
          7 births for which M40G is inconsistent with M37T.
          5 births for which M40H is inconsistent with M37P.

16.  In V437 and V438 (anthropometry for mother), there are a
     number of cases with values well below or above an expected
     value.  The data user may wish to exclude cases for which the
     weight is less than 10 kg or greater than 165 kg or for which
     the height is less than 1 meter.

17.  There are 78 births for which the date of one vaccination in
     a series is less than 7 days after the previous vaccination.

18.  There are 312 births for which H2 (BCG given) and HW14 (BCG
     scar seen) are inconsistent.

19.  There are 426 births for which HW1 (age of child in months)
     and HW15 (measured standing or lying down) are inconsistent.

20.  There are 13 children for whom HW3 (height) is less than 20
     cm.

21.  There are 10 births for which HW13 (result of measurement) is
     not 0 (measured), but a date was present in the data in HW17
     through HW19.  These variables are not applicable in the final
     recode file.

22.  There are 5 respondents who reported age at first marriage as
     less than 10 years.

23.  There are 58 cases for which V525 (age at first sexual
     intercourse) is greater than V212 (age at first birth).

24.  There are 9 cases in which V630A (discussed family planning
     with partner) has a value even though the respondent is not
     currently in union.

25.  There are 23 cases in which V705 (partner's occupation) equals
     4 (agriculture), but V707 (type of land) is not applicable.
     Of these cases, the coding in V704 (occupation not recoded) is
     60 for 16 cases, 65 for 6 cases and 66 for 1 case.  These
     "agricultural" occupations skipped the question about type of
     land.

26.  There were 6 cases in which there was a response for V723
     (caretaker for child), but the youngest living child is
     exactly 4 years of age.  In Benin this question was to be
     limited to women with living children 3 years of age and
     younger.  These 6 cases have been set to not applicable in the
     recode data file.

27.  There are 165 cases for which V730 (partner's age) is more
     than 5 years different from the partner's age reported in the
     household.

28.  There are 29 cases in which V803 (duration of interview) is
     greater than 3 hours.

29.  There is 1 case for which V853 was 0 (no partners other than
     husband) but the respondent had had sex in the last year.
     This case was corrected to "98" (don't know) for V853.  There
     were 2 cases in the raw data for which a response for V853
     existed, but the respondents had not had sex in the last year.
     This response of 0 partners was corrected to NOTAPPL for V853.

Coding Additions

1.   V128 and V129 codes have been extended to include "98", "Don't
     know for visitors", since visitors were not asked the
     questions defined by these two variables.

2.   V511 has a minimum of 8 years of age.

3.   V610 includes the value of "2" (indifferent)

4.   V613, V627, V628, V629 include a code of "95", "God's
     decision".

